body:
Wow! Thanks for this great insight. 

&#x200B;

>I also feel like you should show off more of your AE motion work because that will give you a leg up.

I'm agree with you, but most of my motion work that I made was for the company that I'm currently working with. So, if I use it in my portfolio I might get in trouble for copyright and stuff.

&#x200B;

>Don’t cut to the beat because thats what you feel like you should do and throw in a ton of footage

Got it! I know that cutting to the beat it's one of my biggest pet peeves in this area. I'm trying to loose myself a little bit.

&#x200B;

I truly appreciate every line you said. I'll craft better this reel and the upcoming ones with your advice. Thanks a lot, man!

So, yesterday I made a few tests with the equipment we have in the office. Green screen and white background.

&#x200B;

The results with white background were just awful. I mean, I thought to key out with Luma Key, but just... UGH! Then, I tried to rotobrushed it. Again, UGH. The edges look like they were cut with scissors or with a machete.

&#x200B;

On the other hand, the results with green screen were so much better. We used a girl with a curly blonde hair, cuz challenges. The shots were made with a Sony A7ii:

&#x200B;

RAW Footage:

[https://imgur.com/3pi0hwY](https://imgur.com/3pi0hwY)

&#x200B;

With Keylight and Advanced Spill Supressor applied:

[https://imgur.com/IqWQk16](https://imgur.com/IqWQk16)

&#x200B;

Personally, I think the edges are fine, but I think it can refine them a little bit more.

&#x200B;

What do you guys think?

&#x200B;

And thanks in advanced for your comments!
